<ecore:EPackage xmi:version="2.0" xmlns:xmi="http://www.omg.org/XMI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xmlns:ecore="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" name="calc" nsURI="https://www.daanse.org/spec/org.eclipse.daanse.cwm.model.daanse.resource.calc"
    nsPrefix="calc">
  <eAnnotations source="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore">
    <details key="invocationDelegates" value="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore/OCL"/>
    <details key="settingDelegates" value="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore/OCL"/>
    <details key="validationDelegates" value="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore/OCL"/>
  </eAnnotations>
  <eAnnotations source="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore/OCL">
    <details key="hiddenOpposites" value="true"/>
  </eAnnotations>
  <eAnnotations source="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel">
    <details key="documentation" value="The Calc package describes the structure of spreadsheets - OpenDocument Calc (OASIS ODF, ISO 26300) and OOXML SpreadsheetML (Excel) - and of the data held in them.&#xA;&#xA;What separates a spreadsheet from a CSV file. Both carry column-shaped data, and the individual column is an objectmodel/core::Attribute in either case. Three things are added, though. First, one file contains several sheets. Second, a sheet is a two-dimensional grid on which several mutually independent regions of quite different shape may lie side by side or one below the other - a header block, a table below it, a cross tabulation beside it. Third, position is therefore part of the structure rather than incidental: only the row and column number says which region is meant. A stream of records needs none of this, which is why nothing here inherits from resource/record::RecordDef - its mandatory attributes fieldDelimiter and textDelimiter describe delimiters, whereas in a spreadsheet the grid itself does the separating.&#xA;&#xA;Open-ended regions. A region need not have an end. A database or list table grows downwards as rows are appended, and its last row is not fixed at description time. That is why lastRow and lastColumn on CellRange are unsettable and default to -1: where the value is unset, the region is open in that direction. firstRow and firstColumn, by contrast, are always fixed - a region always has a beginning.&#xA;&#xA;Cell contents. In ODF, table:table-cell carries the content through office:value-type and office:value, the formula through table:formula, and the merging of several cells through table:number-columns-spanned and table:number-rows-spanned; covered cells appear as table:covered-table-cell. In SpreadsheetML the cell stands as c with the A1 address in r and the type code in t, the value in v and the formula in f. Named regions are held by ODF as table:named-range or table:named-expression, and by SpreadsheetML as definedName. Structured regions are called table:database-range in ODF and table (a ListObject) with ref, headerRowCount and totalsRowCount in SpreadsheetML.&#xA;&#xA;Formulas are kept here as text, in the notation of the source file; which notation that is is stated by formulaLanguage on the Workbook. A parsed representation belongs in foundation/expressions rather than here.&#xA;&#xA;Mapping onto CWM. The Workbook is an objectmodel/core::Package and so is the sheet - both are namespaces and carry their content through the inherited ownedElement. A region is an objectmodel/core::Class and carries its columns through the inherited, ordered containment reference Classifier::feature. A row is an objectmodel/instance::Object and a cell an objectmodel/instance::DataSlot - the same pair with which resource/relational maps Row and ColumnValue.&#xA;&#xA;Sources: OASIS Open Document Format for Office Applications v1.2/v1.3 Part 1 (Schema) and Part 4 (OpenFormula); ECMA-376 Office Open XML, Part 1, SpreadsheetML."/>
    <details key="basePackage" value="org.eclipse.daanse.cwm.model.daanse.resource"/>
    <details key="prefix" value="Calc"/>
    <details key="oSGiCompatible" value="true"/>
    <details key="literalsInterface" value="true"/>
    <details key="loadInitialization" value="false"/>
    <details key="copyrightText" value="Copyright (c) 2026 Contributors to the Eclipse Foundation.&#xA;&#xA;This program and the accompanying materials are made&#xA;available under the terms of the Eclipse Public License 2.0&#xA;which is available at https://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-2.0/&#xA;&#xA;SPDX-License-Identifier: EPL-2.0"/>
  </eAnnotations>
